Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] Tests: okhttp's wildcard hostname cases on the platform
 verifier

Jelly Bean counterpart of external_okhttp/0003 of the 4.4 series
("Fix a bug in OkHostnameVerifier wildcard handling", okhttp e82a796):
a pattern *.remainder matched every host name that starts with the
remainder ("*.foo.com" matched "foo.com.au").

Android 4.1-4.3 have no okhttp: HttpsURLConnection's default verifier
is javax.net.ssl.DefaultHostnameVerifier, which OkHostnameVerifier was
copied from and which had the same bug. Its fix is the libcore change
of the same bug (0014 of this series), so there is no code change here.
This adds the wildcard cases of okhttp's HostnameVerifierTest
(android-4.4.2_r2) to DefaultHostnameVerifierTest, with the
"foo.com.au" assertion of e82a796, run through
verify(String, SSLSession) as HttpsURLConnection calls it:
verifyWilcardCn, verifyWilcardCnOnTld, wildcardsCannotMatchIpAddresses,
wildcardsDoesNotNeedTwoDots and subjectAltNameWithWildcard. The
non-ASCII subjectAltName case is @Ignore'd in okhttp as well and is not
ported. The session is a FakeSSLSession that returns the parsed
certificate (the Jelly Bean FakeSSLSession has no certificate
constructor).

Android 4.1 port (android-4.1.2_r2) of patch 0032 of the Android 4.3
series (no 4.4 libcore counterpart: 4.4 has this fix in external_okhttp
0003), made from its Android 4.2 port. 4.1's
DefaultHostnameVerifierTest, like 4.2's, still holds the
DistinguishedNameParser cases that 4.3 moved to their own test: its
DistinguishedNameParser import stays next to the new SSLSession import.
Otherwise only line offsets differ.
